# P040 gate working notes (evidence log, appended per idea; each save is backed up in backups/P040/)
Started 2026-10-18. Format: idea -> searches -> findings (URL) -> tentative verdict.


## 1. Parity (searches done: 7)
- Competitors/analogs: Thought Machine + AWS Transform AI migration (announced 2026-09-29; extracts business intent as EARS specs, consolidates product variants, generates target products + test suites, human sign-off gates; target = Thought Machine Vault only) https://press.aboutamazon.com/aws/2026/9/thought-machine-partners-with-aws-on-ai-powered-solution-to-accelerate-legacy-banking-migrations ; Tweezr + 10x (May 2026; deterministic AI reads legacy source code to recover hidden business rules, dependencies; on-prem; COBOL/Java/C++/PLSQL) https://fintech.global/2026/05/08/10x-banking-and-tweezr-partner-to-speed-core-migration/ ; Mechanical Orchard (>$84M raised by Apr 2025; behavioural-equivalence loop, mainframe) https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250403982657/en/ ; Fiserv + OpenAI agentOS (May 2026) to streamline "core conversions" (secondary source); QualityKiosk/Katalon/ContextQA (testing-as-a-service, parallel-run parity regression); FIntegrate FusionLRS (archives; claims $40-250k typical data conversion cost, up to 70% cheaper) https://www.cuinsight.com/press-release/fintegrate-introduces-fusionlrs-for-legacy-data-conversions-and-research/
- Premise "No independent AI parity product found" is half wrong: vendor-side AI migration tooling now exists at Thought Machine/AWS (target-specific) and Tweezr/10x; AI-native cores (Maximum $30M seed Aug 2026, Emprise Bank) bundle migration. Nobody neutral for incumbent-to-incumbent (e.g., DNA -> Symitar) found.
- Conversion cost reality: $3.6M all-in at $2.8B First Community; $3.7M three-bank holding co; $0.5M Fauquier small bank; Cornerstone: 32 conversions in 2023 (banks+CUs, core/digital/payments mixed) https://www.crnrstone.com/press-release/record-bank-credit-union-technology-conversions . Wipfli: consultant negotiation fees = % of savings. So $350k/conversion = ~10% of all-in at big banks; for sub-$1B bank ~50%+ of the whole budget: not credible for the small end.
- FDIC proposal VERIFIED: board approved 2026-09-17 proposed rule, 90-day standard, 5-day de minimis https://www.skadden.com/insights/publications/2026/09/fdic-issues-proposal-on-bank-merger-transactions . JKHY deconversion fees: $10.9M FY23, $23.0M FY22 (mostly acquired clients) -> deconversion fee pool is real but small (Jack Henry whole company) https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/779152/000077915223000062/jkhy-20230630.htm
- Tentative: weak. Median ~$12M, upside ~$70-90M only with Sunset; vendor AI tooling (Fiserv agentOS, Thought Machine) compresses the same pain from the vendor side.

## 2. Tenancy (searches done: 8)
- Hidden incumbent at exactly this: CU*Answers (Grand Rapids; 310+ staff; 210+ CUs in 34 states as of Dec 2025; owners 130-145; FY2024 total sales $77.9M = CU*BASE $55.8M + CNS/iSeries $13.1M + external $9.0M) https://www.cuanswers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024-fq4-financials-4-page-version.pdf ; FY2025 YTD CU*BASE revenue $65.6M https://www.cuanswers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025-fq4-financials-4-page-version.pdf . So the idea's claim "no CUSO past a few dozen owners" is FALSE: after ~50 years the best shared-core CUSO is ~$78M on 210 CUs (~$370k per CU). Tenancy's $100M = beating the incumbent's all-time scale.
- Back office CUSOs: CU Service Network + Aux (50-55 owners, ~200 participating clients; accounting, CFO, compliance; Aux "doubled accounting staff" for demand; growth 10-12 new clients a quarter in 2021-22) https://news.cuna.org/articles/print/120990-cuso-model-streamlines-back-office-services ; Trellance bought Ongoing Operations (IT) 2022; CU Works Compliance (launched 2017). BSA officer outsourcing specifically: not confirmed offered by CU back-office CUSOs (white space, but also board accountability).
- Pricing reality: CU*Answers list price for core member processing $0.505-0.56/member/month (7-yr), $0.80 1-yr renewal https://www.cuanswers.com/wp-content/uploads/2026_Online_Pricing_Guide.pdf ; tech spend median ~0.35% of assets (Cornerstone). $3-4 PMPM all-in for core+digital+card+lending ops+BSA+accounting+compliance is below what the parts cost to staff; margin story unproven.
- NCUA Q1 2026: 4,250 CUs (from 4,411), 748 > $500M; 639 CUs negative earnings, mostly <$100M https://ncua.gov/newsroom/press-release/2026/ncua-releases-first-quarter-2026-credit-union-system-performance-data . 2025 mergers: 157 approved (162 in 2024; peak 254 in 2019); median target ~ $11M assets; most reason codes "expanded services" (strategic) not distress https://tyfone.com/news/breaking-news/credit-union-mergers-dip-slightly-in-2025-as-industry-consolidation-continues/ . So the CUs that actually die are tiny ($11M median), not the $50-500M segment; the $50-500M pool pays for what it already has.
- Legal: 12 CFR 712 allows service CUSOs (eCFR current to 2026-08-17); NCUA has no direct CUSO authority; registry 1,127 CUSOs (2025) https://ncua.gov/files/publications/cusos-at-a-glance-2025.pdf . Fiserv "1,155 CUs / 25.9% share" claim: could not verify (2009 release: 32% of CU account processing).
- Tentative: weak. Median ~$12M, upside ~$70M (needs to out-scale CU*Answers in 6 yrs; boards slow).

## 3. Quorum (searches done: 3 + shared)
- Existing: Cornerstone Consulting core/contract negotiation (claims proprietary DB of 15,000+ pricing data points; negotiates Fiserv DNA/Signature, FIS Systematics/IBS, JH SilverLake) https://www.crnrstone.com/new-contracts-negotiation ; Paladin fs database (<$5B FIs) + Golden Contract Coalition (launched 2016, Pillsbury, members pay) https://fintechfutures.com/?p=515252 ; BPI 2014 study: 10.2-41.4% over fair market, avg $1.2M saved per 5-yr contract (Paladin data, biased) https://bpinetwork.org/about/media/press-release/45 ; CBANC marketplace sells vendor-paid qualified leads/placement https://www.pymnts.com/?p=1228709 ; Wipfli: consultants paid as % of savings.
- Substitutes: consultants (success fee on savings), peer groups, state bankers associations. Vendor-paid: CBANC/Datos/Bank Director sell attention.
- Math: idea's own admission "likely dead standalone"; 6% x $0.6-1.2B = $36-72M at 100% capture. Realistic capture 10-20% => $4-14M.
- Tentative: kill (small even in upside). Median ~4, upside ~25.

## 4. Sunset (searches done: 5)
- PRIMARY SOURCE KILL SHOT on why-now: Fiserv Investor Day 2026-05-14 (SEC DEFA14A): "commitment to no forced core migrations"; clients "no longer need a large, expensive core conversion to modernize"; core attrition "roughly doubled from 75bp to 150bp", tied to the 2022 consolidation of cores "from 16 to 5"; plan assumes gradual return to historical levels by 2029. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000798354/000119312526226797/d150338ddefa14a.htm . Also MarketBeat 2026-03-19: Lyons "abandoned forced core consolidation". So the vendor tried the sunset the idea sells, it caused doubled churn, and publicly renounced it. The idea's "earned secret" is true but the pain it would remove is exactly what the vendor now says it will not attempt.
- Fiserv Q2 2026 (2026-08-06): new CEO Georgakopoulos (since June), portfolio review of non-best-in-class products (debit networks in scope), Project Elevate >=$500M savings, centralising product/tech org; guide -1% to flat organic. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000798354/000079835426000028/fisvq226earningsrelease.htm . Portfolio review = divestitures, not migrations.
- Other vendors: FIS has OpenCoreOS (AI-native core, bought Mar 2026) and Modern Banking Platform, builds in-house; Jack Henry: gradual public-cloud Platform, "at your own pace". No FIS/JH sunset program found. 
- Substitutes: vendors' own conversion teams; SIs (Cognizant, Infosys, Tata); Fiserv-OpenAI agentOS to streamline conversions (secondary); Thought Machine/AWS.
- Tentative: kill (false premise on why-now; one buyer; vendor in-sourcing AI). Median ~5, upside ~40.

## 5. Books of Record (searches done: 6)
- Hidden/funded competitors in "real-time ledger beside the batch core": Finzly BankOS (sits beside cores; Account Galaxy = 24/7 real-time ledger; Payment Galaxy hub for ACH/wire/FedNow/RTP; Frost Bank for FedNow+RTP) https://ffnews.com/news/finzly-joins-american-fintech-council-to-drive-composable-banking-and-payment-modernizat-b03523dc ; Volante ($116M total raised, $66M round Sixth Street, targeting mid-tier banks; unified FedNow+RTP) https://finextra.com/newsarticle/43238/volante-raises-66m ; Percipient Twinn digital twin (acquired by Accenture 2025-01-03) https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250103552551/en ; Episode Six ParallelLedger (sidecar ledger) https://episodesix.com/solutions/parallelledger ; DCI sidecar core (Texas National), Nymbus Launch, Neocova (two banks funded), Fiserv Finxact (Thread Bank, Flagstar phased migration), FIS bought OpenCoreOS (Mar 2026). "No neutral mirror found" is overstated: sidecar ledger/hub is a populated category; the unoccupied part is only "vendor-neutral, bank-owned journal replica", which is also exactly what a stronger sidecar can add.
- Demand check: FedNow ~1,800 participants (July 2026, only ~20.7% of ~8,500 small FIs; small CUs 13.6%); Q2 2026 settled payments 4.997M ($274.7B), 2025 full year 8.41M payments; RTP Q1 2026 128M txns. Stand-in value is therefore tiny today: 5M payments a quarter across ~1,800 FIs is ~30 payments per FI per day. https://frbservices.org/resources/financial-services/fednow/quarterly-volume-value-stats ; https://www.digitaltransactions.net/fednow-marks-a-third-anniversary-tally-at-more-than-1800-banks-and-credit-unions/
- Resilience substitute: Sheltered Harbor (FS-ISAC, voluntary; fees $250-$25,000 for non-large banks, 2017 data) already covers the "vendor outage / board wants a data copy" use case at very low price https://bankingjournal.aba.com/2017/03/how-sheltered-harbor-provides-safety-from-the-cyber-storm/
- Tech premise unverified by author: whether cores expose journals/event streams; vendors charge for extracts. Also real-time OFAC/AML in 5 seconds is a compliance task a ledger copy does not solve.
- Tentative: weak. Median ~$10M, upside ~$55M (becoming a core = Nymbus/Finxact/Thought Machine territory, and requires a bank to trust a startup with ledger of record).

## 6. Day One (searches done: 6)
- Curinos benchmark verified: avg branch-based deposit attrition 11.1% Legal Day 1 to 3 months post-conversion; spread >12 pts; bottom quartile >17%, top <5%; high-risk high-value customers targeted pre-conversion had materially lower attrition (so predictive retention analytics is already sold by Curinos) https://www.curinos.com/insights/curinos-perspective-generating-value-bank-m-and-a-fails ; https://curinos.com/our-insights/curinos-review-q126-every-deal-deposit-retention-strategy/
- Hidden competitors on the "invisible connections" leg: Pinwheel (deposit switching + bill switching incl. card-on-file; raised $50M Series B ~Apr 2024, ~$77M total per one profile; integrated with Q2 (Apr 2025), Candescent, Narmi, Bankjoy (70+ FIs), MEA Financial; Varo pilot: 40% of users who linked externals switched >=1 bill) https://www.pinwheelapi.com/bill-switch/learn-more ; Argyle, Atomic (CoAuth, claims ~28M Americans); Alkami Digital Banking Conversion Toolkit (Sep 2025) https://www.cuinsight.com/press-release/alkami-launches-first-to-market-research-backed-digital-banking-conversion-toolkit-for-financial-institution-leaders/ ; Visa Account Updater / Mastercard ABU + network tokens handle card-on-file when issuer participates (Lloyds migration an example of a skipped updater).
- Legal: CFPB 1033 rule enjoined (Forcht Bank v. CFPB, E.D. Ky.) and under reconsideration (ANPRM Aug 22 2025; compliance dates effectively suspended) https://www.dlapiper.com/en/insights/publications/2025/08/cfpb-rulemaking-on-personal-financial-data-rights : so idea's "no right to pull data" is right; runs on bank authority + consent.
- Sizing: idea itself says M&A-only version is $20-40M and $100M needs 25% of ~1,000-1,400 events plus a second leg (primary switching at $12/switch x 3M = $36M) that Pinwheel/Argyle/Atomic already serve. Merger targets skew tiny (median CU target ~$11M assets) so $250k per deal fits only the bank deals >~$500M (maybe 100/yr).
- Causal claim (invisible connections drive attrition) is the author's inference; Curinos attributes to convergence disruption broadly. Needs decomposition test; cheapest test is real.
- Tentative: weak. Median ~9, upside ~45.
